# the argument of aquinas-ontological-objection.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Aquinas, ST I q.2 a.1, Objection 2: "God exists" is self-evident
# S3 (whole greater than its part) is only an illustration and is left out.
atom	known_from_terms	the proposition "God exists" is known as soon as its terms are known, i.e. as soon as the signification of the word "God" is understood
atom	self_evident	the proposition "God exists" is self-evident
atom	first_principles_known_from_terms	the first principles of demonstration are known as soon as their terms are known
atom	god_means_greatest	by the word "God" is signified that thing than which nothing greater can be conceived
atom	actual_greater	that which exists actually and mentally is greater than that which exists only mentally
atom	understood	the word "God" is understood
atom	exists_mentally	God exists mentally
atom	exists_actually	God exists actually
P1	S2	known_from_terms → self_evident	kind conceptual
P2	S2	first_principles_known_from_terms	says aristotle
P3	S5	god_means_greatest	kind conceptual
P4	S6	actual_greater
P5	S7	understood → exists_mentally
# The step in S7 relies, unstated, on: if God means that than which nothing greater can be conceived, and actual-and-mental existence is greater than mental existence alone, then God's existing mentally entails God's existing actually.
A1	-	god_means_greatest ∧ actual_greater ∧ exists_mentally → exists_actually
C1	S7	understood → exists_actually	from P3 P4 P5 A1
# The move from C1 to S4 relies, unstated, on: if understanding the word suffices for God's existing actually, then "God exists" is known as soon as its terms are known.
A2	-	(understood → exists_actually) → known_from_terms
C2	S4	known_from_terms	from C1 A2
C	S8	self_evident	from P1 C2
